    <text xml:lang="ta" type="edition">
      <body>
        <div rend="parallel">
            <p xml:id="Aink443-kilavi">ituvumatu.</p>
            <p xml:lang="en">And this is that.</p>
              </div>
        <div rend="parallel">
          <lg xml:id="Aink443">        
            <l>naṉicēyt teṉṉātu naṟṟē rēṟicceṉ</l>
            <l>ṟilaṅku nilavi ṉiḷampiṟai pōlak</l>
            <l>kāṇkuven tillavavaḷ kaviṉpeṟu cuṭarnutal</l>
            <l>viṇṇuya raraṇpala vauviya</l>
            <l>maṇṇuṟu muraciṉ vēntutoḻil viṭiṉē.</l>
        </lg>  
 <lg xml:lang="en">                    
  <l>If the king of the clay[-topped]<note xml:lang="en" place="foot">I supply 'topped' here as it is unclear why or where the earth has been applied to the drum, and in absence of a better idea, I follow Turacāmi Piḷḷai's modern commentary.</note> drum,</l>
  <l>who seized many sky-high fortresses,</l>
  <l>releases [me] from the task, then, without saying, 'It is very far',</l>
  <l>would that I might<note xml:lang="en" place="foot">This is my attempt to render the force of the particle <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">-tilla</foreign></note> mount the good chariot, go, and see the bright forehead</l>
  <l>that obtains beauty like the young crescent moon in shining moonlight.<note xml:lang="en" place="foot">Is the hero's return the cause of the heroine's shining forehead, or is this simply its innate quality -- one that is activated in the glow of the moon?</note></l>

</lg>                      
        </div>
      </body>
